erminate involuntary pregnancies.\n\nWe will continue promoting full sexual and reproductive health care and rights in every context, in the Security Council, other UN bodies and elsewhere. Earlier health provisions—paragraph 19 of resolution 2106 (2013) and paragraph 10 of resolution 1889 (2009)—remain in the reaffirmed framework; their omission does not erase them. This adopted resolution does not establish our support for, or a recorded vote on, an unspecified future text or a future vote. We remain committed to continuing the fight against sexual violence in conflict.<|im_end|>", "output_display": "France welcomes the adoption of Germany-initiated resolution 2467 (2019), a milestone rather than the end of our effort. The strengthened mandate for the Special Representative on Sexual Violence in Conflict, Ms. Pramila Patten, and a survivor-centred approach are important advances. The requested Secretary-General report on children born of rape by 2021 matters.\n\nConcessions were made under pressure from several permanent members. The removal of previously agreed sexual and reproductive health language—agreed in resolutions 1889 (2009) and 2106 (2013)—shocks us. Permanent-member threats of a veto challenge 25 years of progress. Victims need sexual and reproductive health care; the omission undermines women's dignity. Women must decide freely about their bodies, including choosing to terminate involuntary pregnancies.\n\nWe will continue promoting full sexual and reproductive health care and rights in every context, in the Security Council, other UN bodies and elsewhere. Earlier health provisions—paragraph 19 of resolution 2106 (2013) and paragraph 10 of resolution 1889 (2009)—remain in the reaffirmed framework; their omission does not erase them. This adopted resolution does not establish our support for, or a recorded vote on, an unspecified future text or a future vote. We remain committed to continuing the fight against sexual violence in conflict.", "generated_